SPU: lifted code built as a module queues its transfers through a slot pointer cached in the context.

With SPU_MODULE_ACCESSORS every `g_spu_trampoline_fn = f` of the lift (683 in one Resistance 3 job module) called
the host's spu_tramp_slot() through a dyld stub, which asked _tlv_get_addr for an address that never changes for
the thread: ~1.5-2% of the SPU threads' time in a firefight (`sample`: spu_tramp_slot + its stub ~110, its
_tlv_get_addr ~55 of ~9-12k). spu_context.tramp_slot now holds the running thread's slot: spu_run_with_halt sets
it, every lift reaches it there (a context runs on one host thread), and the module writes through it, falling
back to the accessor while it is NULL (a context lifted code reaches outside spu_run_with_halt). spu_drain_call
compares it with its thread's slot on entry and rebinds it with a log line if another thread left it. Without
SPU_MODULE_ACCESSORS nothing changes but the field.

Test: tools/test_spu_module_tramp_slot.py (runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_module_tramp_slot.c with a "module" TU built
with SPU_MODULE_ACCESSORS: the accessor without a slot, the slot when set, the binding by spu_run_with_halt on this
and on another thread, the rebinding by spu_drain_call; 7 checks, each of the three host changes left out fails
some). Micro-benchmark (a module-built function re-queued through spu_drain_call, static link): 7.5-7.8 ->
6.8-7.2 ns per step.
In the game (`sample`, 25 s of the Haven firefight, 2 runs each): spu_tramp_slot + stub 1.6% -> none of the SPU
threads' samples, _tlv_get_addr 1.6-2.0% -> 1.1-1.4%; no rebinding was logged.

Upstream: not proposed. Same as 0001.
